re-wrap">Here is a refined and structured implementation of a CORBA-based distributed
calculator that demonstrates object brokering. This version improves code clarity, error
handling, and deployment steps for easy execution.

Steps to Implement a Distributed Calculator using
CORBA
1. Define the IDL (Interface Definition Language) file for the Calculator.
2. Generate CORBA Stubs and Skeletons using IDL Compiler.
3. Implement the CORBA Server to register the remote Calculator object.
4. Implement the CORBA Client to invoke the remote object’s methods.

5. Run the ORB (Object Request Broker) Naming Service and execute the Client-
Server.

1⃣ Define the IDL File (Calculator.idl)
The IDL (Interface Definition Language) describes the Calculator interface:
module CalculatorApp {
interface Calculator {
float add(in float a, in float b);
float subtract(in float a, in float b);
float multiply(in float a, in float b);
float divide(in float a, in float b) raises(DivisionByZero);
};
exception DivisionByZero {};
};
Save this as Calculator.idl.
The DivisionByZero exception ensures safe division operations.

2⃣ Generate CORBA Stubs and Skeletons
After writing Calculator.idl, compile it using:
idlj -fall Calculator.idl
This generates CalculatorApp package with helper classes:
 Calculator.java
 CalculatorHelper.java
 CalculatorPOA.java (for server-side implementation)

3⃣ Implement the CORBA Server (CalculatorServer.java)
The server creates a CORBA object and binds it to the ORB's naming service.
import CalculatorApp.*;
import org.omg.CORBA.*;
import org.omg.CosNaming.*;
import org.omg.PortableServer.*;
class CalculatorImpl extends CalculatorPOA {
private ORB orb;
public void setORB(ORB orb_val) {
orb = orb_val;
}
public float add(float a, float b) {
return a + b;
}
public float subtract(float a, float b) {
return a - b;
}
public float multiply(float a, float b) {
return a * b;
}
public float divide(float a, float b) throws DivisionByZero {
if (b == 0) throw new DivisionByZero();
return a / b;
}
}
public class CalculatorServer {
public static void main(String args[]) {
try {
// Initialize ORB
ORB orb = ORB.init(args, null);
// Get reference to RootPOA and activate POA Manager
POA rootpoa =
POAHelper.narrow(orb.resolve_initial_references("RootPOA"));
rootpoa.the_POAManager().activate();
// Create servant and register it with ORB
CalculatorImpl calculator = new CalculatorImpl();
calculator.setORB(orb);
org.omg.CORBA.Object ref =
rootpoa.servant_to_reference(calculator);
Calculator href = CalculatorHelper.narrow(ref);
// Bind object to Naming Service
org.omg.CORBA.Object objRef =
orb.resolve_initial_references("NameService");
NamingContextExt ncRef = NamingContextExtHelper.narrow(objRef);
NameComponent path[] = ncRef.to_name("Calculator");

ncRef.rebind(path, href);
System.out.println(" Calculator Server is running...");
orb.run();
} catch (Exception e) {
System.err.println(" Server exception: " + e.getMessage());
e.printStackTrace();
}
}
}
Improvements:
Exception Handling added for robustness.
Naming Service Binding ensures clients can locate the Calculator.
ORB Activation and POA Management added for smooth operation.

4⃣ Implement the CORBA Client (CalculatorClient.java)
The client connects to the Naming Service, locates the Calculator, and invokes remote
methods.
import CalculatorApp.*;
import org.omg.CORBA.*;
import org.omg.CosNaming.*;
public class CalculatorClient {
public static void main(String args[]) {
try {
// Initialize ORB
ORB orb = ORB.init(args, null);
// Get reference to Naming Service
org.omg.CORBA.Object objRef =
orb.resolve_initial_references("NameService");
NamingContextExt ncRef = NamingContextExtHelper.narrow(objRef);
// Resolve Calculator object
Calculator calculator =
CalculatorHelper.narrow(ncRef.resolve_str("Calculator"));
// Perform operations
System.out.println("Addition (10 + 5): " + calculator.add(10,
5));
System.out.println("Subtraction (10 - 5): " +
calculator.subtract(10, 5));
System.out.println("Multiplication (10 * 5): " +
calculator.multiply(10, 5));
try {
System.out.println("Division (10 / 2): " +
calculator.divide(10, 2));
System.out.println("Division (10 / 0): " +
calculator.divide(10, 0)); // Should raise exception
} catch (DivisionByZero e) {

System.out.println("⚠ Error: Division by zero is not
allowed!");
}
} catch (Exception e) {
System.err.println(" Client exception: " + e.getMessage());
e.printStackTrace();
}
}
}
Improvements:
Handles Division by Zero Exception.
User-friendly error messages for better debugging.
ORB Initialization and Service Resolution Optimized.

5⃣ Compile and Run the CORBA Application
Step 1: Compile the IDL file
idlj -fall Calculator.idl
Step 2: Compile Java files
javac CalculatorServer.java CalculatorClient.java CalculatorApp/*.java
Step 3: Start the ORB Naming Service
tnameserv -ORBInitialPort 1050 &amp;
This starts the CORBA Naming Service on port 1050.
Step 4: Run the CORBA Server
java CalculatorServer -ORBInitialPort 1050
You should see:
Calculator Server is running...
Step 5: Run the CORBA Client
java CalculatorClient -ORBInitialPort 1050
Expected Output:
Addition (10 + 5): 15.0
Subtraction (10 - 5): 5.0
Multiplication (10 * 5): 50.0

Division (10 / 2): 5.0
⚠ Error: Division by zero is not allowed!

Summary of Refinements
Proper exception handling (handles division by zero).
Improved CORBA Naming Service binding for better connectivity.
Optimized CORBA object references for seamless communication.
Structured logging and error messages for clarity.
This distributed CORBA-based Calculator successfully demonstrates Object Brokering
in a real-world setup.

