What is CORBA (Common Object Request Broker
Architecture)?
CORBA (Common Object Request Broker Architecture) is a distributed computing
framework developed by OMG (Object Management Group) that enables applications to
communicate with each other over a network, regardless of programming language or
operating system.
CORBA allows different applications written in Java, C++, Python, etc. to communicate
seamlessly.
It is based on the concept of Object Brokering, where an Object Request Broker
(ORB) helps clients and servers communicate transparently.

Key Features of CORBA
Language Independence: Supports multiple languages (Java, C++, Python, etc.).
Platform Independence: Works across different operating systems (Windows, Linux,
macOS).
Object-Oriented Communication: Enables remote method calls on distributed objects.
Interoperability: Applications written in different languages can talk to each other.
Scalability: Used in enterprise-level applications and distributed systems.

How CORBA Works?
CORBA follows a client-server model where the Object Request Broker (ORB) acts as a
middleman that enables communication.
1⃣ Client:
 The client invokes a method on a remote object.
 The Stub (proxy) translates the request and sends it to the ORB.
2⃣ ORB (Object Request Broker):
 The ORB receives the request and forwards it to the correct server object.
 The ORB provides location transparency, meaning the client doesn’t need to know
where the server is.
3⃣ Server:
 The Skeleton (server-side handler) receives the request.

 The actual server object executes the method and sends the result back through the
ORB.

CORBA Architecture
CORBA consists of the following main components:

Component Description

ORB (Object Request Broker) The middleware that handles communication between

clients and servers.

IDL (Interface Definition
Language) Defines the structure of remote objects and their methods.
Stub (Client-Side Proxy) Converts method calls into network requests.
Skeleton (Server-Side
Handler)

Unpacks client requests and invokes methods on the actual
object.

Naming Service Registers and locates CORBA objects dynamically.

Example: How CORBA is Used?
Suppose a Java client wants to communicate with a C++ server over the network.
The client calls a remote method on an object using CORBA.
CORBA's ORB routes the request to the server (written in C++).
The server processes the request and sends back the response.

Real-World Applications of CORBA
Used in banking, telecommunications, healthcare, and aerospace systems where
different applications need to communicate reliably.
Example:
Air Traffic Control Systems use CORBA to integrate different radar and
communication systems.
Telecom Networks use CORBA to manage call routing across different networks.

Summary
CORBA is a middleware framework that enables language-independent and
platform-independent communication in distributed systems.
It uses an Object Request Broker (ORB) to mediate between clients and servers.
Key Components: ORB, IDL, Stub, Skeleton, Naming Service.

Applications: Used in large-scale enterprise and real-time systems like banking,
telecom, and aerospace.
CORBA is a powerful tool for distributed computing! Let me know if you need more
details.

Stub and Skeleton in CORBA
In CORBA (Common Object Request Broker Architecture), stubs and skeletons are
essential components that enable communication between clients and servers in a
distributed system. They act as intermediaries, handling the conversion of method calls and
responses over the network.

1⃣ Stub (Client-Side)
A Stub is a proxy (local representation) of the remote object on the client-side.
It provides the same interface as the remote object but forwards method calls to the
server via CORBA’s ORB (Object Request Broker).
The stub marshals (serializes) the method parameters before sending them to the
server.
Example
When the client calls:
calculator.add(10, 5);
The stub:
 Converts 10 and 5 into a CORBA-compatible request.
 Sends it to the server via the ORB.
 Waits for a response and then returns the result to the client.

2⃣ Skeleton (Server-Side)
A Skeleton is the server-side counterpart of the stub.
It receives the client’s request, unpacks (unmarshals) the parameters, and invokes

the actual method on the remote object.
It then sends the result back to the client.
Example
The Skeleton:
 Receives the marshaled request from the ORB.
 Converts it into a method call:
 float result = calculatorImpl.add(10, 5);
 Sends the result (15.0) back to the client.

How Stub &amp; Skeleton Work in CORBA
Client Request Flow
1⃣ Client calls calculator.add(10, 5);
2⃣ The Stub converts the request into a CORBA message and sends it to the ORB.
3⃣ ORB routes the request to the Skeleton on the server.
4⃣ The Skeleton unmarshals the request and calls calculatorImpl.add(10, 5);
5⃣ The Skeleton sends the result back to the Stub.
6⃣ The Stub unmarshals the result and returns it to the client.

Generating Stubs &amp; Skeletons in CORBA
When we compile an IDL file, CORBA automatically generates the Stub and Skeleton
code:
idlj -fall Calculator.idl
This produces:
 Stub (Client-Side Proxy): CalculatorHelper.java
 Skeleton (Server-Side Handler): CalculatorPOA.java

Summary
Component Location Role
Stub Client-Side Sends method requests to the server
Skeleton Server-Side Receives client requests and invokes the actual method
Stubs and Skeletons enable remote method calls in a CORBA-based distributed
application!</div></div><label for="_r_q2_" class="A_HxFq_toggleControl select-none" data-testid="collapsible-user-message-toggle"><span class="A_HxFq_showMoreLabel">Show more</span><span class="A_HxFq_showLessLabel">Show less</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true" class="A_HxFq_toggleIcon"><use href="/cdn/assets/sprites-core-6d2147a0.svg#ba3792" fill="currentColor"></use></svg></label></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="z-0 flex justify-end"><div aria-label="Your message actions" class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none touch:pointer-events-auto touch:opacity-100 duration-300 group-hover/turn-messages:delay-300 pointer-events-none opacity-0 motion-safe:transition-opacity group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:opacity-100 group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:opacity-100 has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:opacity-100" role="group" tabindex="-1" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy message" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" class="icon"><use href="/cdn/assets/sprites-core-6d2147a0.svg#ce3544" fill="currentColor"></use></svg></span></button><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Edit message" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" class="icon"><use href="/cdn/assets/sprites-core-6d2147a0.svg#6d87e1" fill="currentColor"></use></svg></span></button></div></d