x" data-testid="collapsible-user-message-toggle-checkbox" type="checkbox"><div id="_r_121_" data-testid="collapsible-user-message-content" class="A_HxFq_content"><div class="max-w-full min-w-0 [overflow-wrap:anywhere] whitespace-pre-wrap">Assignment No 7

Title: Create a Simple Web Service and Write a Distributed Application to Consume the
Web Service
Objectives:
 To understand the fundamentals of web services and distributed applications.
 To learn how to design and implement a basic web service using modern web
technologies.
 To explore how distributed systems communicate over a network.
 To develop a client application that consumes a web service using appropriate
protocols.
 To gain hands-on experience in integrating different components of a distributed
architecture.
Problem Statement:
Develop a simple web service that provides basic data or functionality (e.g., a weather
service, user information, or calculator operations). Then, create a distributed application
(web or desktop-based) that consumes this web service and displays or processes the data
returned from it. The service should be capable of handling multiple requests and must follow
standard web protocols such as HTTP/HTTPS.
Expected Outcomes:
 A functional web service (e.g., REST API) implemented using a backend technology
such as Node.js, Flask, or Spring Boot.
 A distributed client application (e.g., in React, Angular, Java, or .NET) that sends
requests to the web service and processes the responses.
 Understanding of client-server interaction, HTTP methods (GET, POST, etc.),
JSON/XML data formats, and asynchronous communication.
 Deployment of the service and client either locally or on cloud platforms such as
Heroku, Render, or Firebase.
 Documentation of endpoints, client-side integration, and testing.

Theory:
1. Introduction to Web Services:
A web service is a software system designed to support interoperable machine-to-machine
interaction over a network. It enables different applications, possibly written in different
languages and running on different platforms, to communicate and exchange data through the
internet using standard protocols such as HTTP, XML, and JSON.

Web services are a core component of modern software development, especially in
distributed systems where components are spread across multiple systems or locations but
need to work together seamlessly.
There are two main types of web services:
 SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol): An XML-based protocol that follows strict
standards and is often used in enterprise environments.
 REST (Representational State Transfer): A lightweight architecture style using
HTTP methods and commonly used with JSON, making it popular for web and
mobile applications.
For this assignment, we typically focus on RESTful web services due to their simplicity and
wide adoption.
2. What is a Distributed Application?
A distributed application is a software application that operates on multiple computers
within a network simultaneously. It is designed so that different components of the
application run on different machines but interact with each other to function as a single
system.
Client-server architecture is a common model of distributed applications:
 The server provides resources or services.
 The client consumes those resources or services.
In this:
 The web service acts as the server, hosting APIs that expose certain functionality
(like a calculator or a weather service).
 The client application is the consumer, making HTTP requests and processing the
server’s response.
3. Components Involved:
 Web Server: Hosts the web service; handles incoming HTTP requests.
 API (Application Programming Interface): A set of defined rules for how the client
communicates with the server. REST APIs use endpoints (URLs) for each operation.
 Client: A software interface, such as a web or desktop application, that consumes the
data or functionality exposed by the web service.
 Data Format: Typically, RESTful services use JSON (JavaScript Object Notation)
because of its lightweight and easy-to-read structure.
4. HTTP Methods in REST APIs:
 GET: Retrieve data from the server.
 POST: Send data to the server (e.g., form submission).
 PUT: Update existing data.

 DELETE: Remove data from the server.
Each method is mapped to an endpoint that performs a specific action.
5. Workflow of the Assignment:
1. Create the Web Service:
o Define routes/endpoints for your service (e.g., /add, /get-user).
o Implement logic for each route (e.g., arithmetic calculations, returning
static/dynamic data).
o Start the web server to listen for client requests.
2. Test the Service:
o Use tools like Postman or a web browser to test your service.
o Ensure endpoints are working as expected and returning the correct response
codes (200 OK, 404 Not Found, etc.).
3. Create the Distributed Client Application:
o Develop a user interface that allows interaction with the web service.
o Write logic to make HTTP requests using libraries like fetch, axios
(JavaScript), or HttpClient (Java/C#).
o Display the server’s response to the user.
4. Integration:
o Ensure the client and server are running on appropriate ports.
o Test the complete flow: client request → server processing → client response
handling.

6. Real-World Use Cases of Web Services and Distributed Systems:
 Online shopping platforms use distributed services for inventory, payments, and
recommendations.
 Mobile apps fetch user data or notifications from backend web services.
 Cloud services expose APIs for storage, machine learning, and analytics.
7. Benefits of Using Web Services in Distributed Applications:
 Scalability: Services can be scaled independently based on demand.
 Interoperability: Systems developed in different languages can communicate.
 Reusability: Services can be reused across multiple applications.
 Maintainability: Easier to debug and update modular services.

Software Requirements:
 Backend (Web Service):
o Programming language: Python (Flask) / JavaScript (Node.js with Express) /
Java (Spring Boot)
o API testing tool: Postman or cURL

o Web server: Apache / Nginx (optional for deployment)
 Frontend or Client Application:
o JavaScript Framework: React / Angular / Vue.js
o OR Desktop Application: JavaFX / .NET (C#) / Electron
 Others:
o Code editor: VS Code / IntelliJ IDEA / Eclipse
o Version control: Git and GitHub
o Web browser for testing
Hardware Requirements:
 Processor: Intel i3 or above
 RAM: Minimum 4 GB
 Storage: Minimum 10 GB free disk space
 Network: Internet connectivity for API calls and testing distributed setup

